sections.s2_technical:
The root cause is that code and data have different lifecycles. When you redeploy, the code reverts instantly and completely. The data does not revert — every row the new version wrote while it was live stays in the new format. So the old binary, which assumes the old format, now reads rows that violate its assumptions. The failure can be loud, like a crash on a missing column or a parse error, or it can be quiet and far worse, like reading a field whose meaning changed and silently producing wrong financial results. A quiet failure after a rollback is especially dangerous because the team believes they are back to a known-good state while the system is actually corrupting data.

```java
// New version writes amount as integer paise in a new column and stops using the old one.
// Old binary expects the old decimal column:
BigDecimal amount = row.getBigDecimal("amount");   // old code reads this
// But new rows have amount=NULL and amount_minor=50000 → old code sees NULL or 0 → wrong
```

Expand-and-contract solves this by never having a moment where only one format exists during the risky window. The expand phase adds the new format without removing the old. On the schema side this means additive changes only — add the new column or table, build indexes online — and crucially the application dual-writes: every change is written in both the old and the new format. Reads can continue from the old format. Because the old format is still being maintained, the old binary can be redeployed at any time and will read correct data.

```java
// EXPAND phase: additive schema + dual-write keeps BOTH formats valid
void applyAmount(Row row, BigDecimal amount) {
    row.setAmount(amount);                                   // OLD format — keep writing!
    row.setAmountMinor(amount.movePointRight(2).longValueExact()); // NEW format
}
// Now either binary can run: old reads amount, new reads amount_minor
```

The coexistence phase is the heart of safety. During this period, which can last days or weeks, the system runs the new code but keeps producing the old format, and you verify continuously that the two formats agree. If the new version has a bug, you roll back to the old binary and it just works, because the old format was never abandoned. You also backfill historical rows into the new format during this phase, in throttled restartable batches, so that eventually both formats are complete for all data. Only when you have high confidence — the new version has run cleanly, verification shows perfect agreement, and you are certain you will not need to roll back — do you enter the contract phase.

The contract phase removes the old format: stop dual-writing, drop the old column, and switch reads fully to the new format. This is the one irreversible step, and it is deliberately done as a separate, much later deployment, long after the risky rollout, precisely because it is the moment rollback stops being safe. A disciplined team treats the contract as a distinct change with its own review, because conflating it with the initial rollout is what recreates the trap.

```java
// CONTRACT phase (separate, much later deploy) — only after you will NOT roll back
void applyAmount(Row row, BigDecimal amount) {
    row.setAmountMinor(amount.movePointRight(2).longValueExact()); // NEW only
    // row.setAmount(...) removed; ALTER TABLE DROP COLUMN amount executed earlier
}
```

The senior-level nuance is that expand-and-contract applies not just to database schemas but to every contract the service exposes — API request and response shapes, event schemas, file formats, and cache entry layouts. The same three phases apply: expand by adding the new shape while keeping the old, coexist and migrate, contract by removing the old only after full confidence. The discipline forces you to always have a window during which both the previous and the current version can operate against the same state, and that window is exactly what makes rollback a real option rather than a hope. The cost is temporary duplication — two formats, dual writes, extra verification — and that cost is the price of being able to undo a bad deploy without data loss, which for a bank is non-negotiable.

